item.text:
Wave 1: roughly 30 candidates screened, 14 documents opened or inspected, 10 accepted source-claim units, 6 duplicates collapsed, and 4 access blockers/timeouts on Kremlin/government pages. The main new material is direct 2022 Putin-linked family support, schools/health funding, and independent UNICEF/WHO/Rosstat baseline evidence.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Instructions following a meeting on socioeconomic support for regions","publisher":"President of Russia","publication_date":"2022-04-10","url":"https://en.kremlin.ru/acts/assignments/orders/copy/68175","claim":"Putin approved instructions to assess unemployed families' financial status so social support could be provided, improve access to support for families with children whose income had fallen, add extra indexation for pensions, social benefits and wages at state and municipal organizations, raise the minimum wage and subsistence minimum, and allocate federal funds to reduce poverty and income inequality in 2022.","locator":"search snippet / presidential instructions summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential instructions published on the Kremlin site with concrete 2022 policy mandates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Putin instruction, with implementation delegated to the government and regions.","contrary_evidence":["The same instructions were largely crisis-response measures under war and sanctions pressure, not evidence of preplanned prosperity building."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Government indexed pensions, the subsistence minimum and the minimum wage by 10%","publisher":"Government of Russia","publication_date":"2022-05-30","url":"https://government.ru/docs/45559/","claim":"The Russian government said that from 1 June 2022 insurance pensions for non-working pensioners, the subsistence minimum and the minimum wage would all rise by 10%, and that the decision was taken on Putin's instruction.","locator":"government announcement headline and decree summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Formal government decree summary with explicit rate change and attribution to Putin's instruction.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Putin-linked policy direction, implemented through the government.","contrary_evidence":["A flat indexation compensates only partly for inflation and does not by itself show lasting welfare improvement."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Government meeting: new monthly benefits for children aged 8-17 from low-income families","publisher":"Government of Russia","publication_date":"2022-05-11","url":"https://government.ru/en/news/45376/","claim":"The government said it had started paying new monthly benefits for children aged 8-17 in low-income families on Putin's instruction, with more than 2.5 million applications covering 3.5 million children and average monthly payments of 6,000 to 12,000 roubles.","locator":"government meeting summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government announcement with concrete uptake and payment details tied to a presidential instruction.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Putin instruction, operationalized through government payment systems.","contrary_evidence":["The program is targeted to low-income families and does not by itself resolve broader child poverty or service-quality problems."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.7","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Government allocated over 35.3 billion roubles for families with children","publisher":"Government of Russia","publication_date":"2022-10-13","url":"https://government.ru/dep_news/46773/","claim":"The government said more than 35.3 billion roubles would be additionally sent to regions for monthly payments to low-income and large families with children.","locator":"government news summary on 11 October 2022 directives","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official budget-allocation announcement with a specific funding amount and beneficiary group.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but clear Putin-period attribution through the federal executive branch.","contrary_evidence":["Extra transfers can cushion hardship without proving durable gains in underlying living standards."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Government meeting: payments to hero mothers and parental glory recipients","publisher":"Government of Russia","publication_date":"2022-09-29","url":"https://government.ru/en/news/46651/","claim":"The government said President Putin revived the Soviet-era Hero Mother title with a 1 million rouble payment, raised the Order of Parental Glory payment to 500,000 roubles, and added 200,000 roubles for the Parental Glory medal.","locator":"government meeting summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government meeting summary with explicit benefit terms and direct presidential initiative.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Putin-linked family-policy decision.","contrary_evidence":["This is pro-natalist spending, not a broad measure of child well-being or service quality."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.7","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Government supports school meals and child-health measures","publisher":"Government of Russia","publication_date":"2022-01-27","url":"https://government.ru/news/44251/","claim":"By presidential instruction, the government said primary-school pupils receive free hot meals and that almost 59 billion roubles was spent in 2022, with nearly 188 billion roubles allocated for 2022-2024; the same package included anti-obesity measures and public posting of school meal information.","locator":"government news summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government announcement with spending figures and implementation details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but substantial Putin-period policy attribution through the executive branch.","contrary_evidence":["The policy is school-specific and does not by itself establish broader system quality or equity."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Government says at least 1,300 new schools will be built","publisher":"Government of Russia","publication_date":"2022-03-24","url":"https://government.ru/en/news/45073/","claim":"The government said that 324 new kindergartens had opened, about 100 new schools had opened, and that at least 1,300 new secondary schools would be built by 2025; it also said more than 4,000 school buses had been purchased and free hot meals were being provided to primary school students.","locator":"government news summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official implementation summary with concrete delivery numbers and an explicit state program frame.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022, with implementation carried over from Putin-era education policy.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect ruler attribution through the federal executive and presidential initiative.","contrary_evidence":["The target extends beyond 2022 and combines older pipeline projects with new construction, so attribution to the single year is partial."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child-Related SDG Progress Assessment for Russian Federation","publisher":"UNICEF Data","publication_date":"2026-07-19","url":"https://data.unicef.org/sdgs/country/rus/","claim":"UNICEF's Russia dashboard reports 97% basic drinking water access in 2022, 89% basic sanitation access in 2022, 76% safely managed drinking water, 61% safely managed sanitation, and 0% open defecation.","locator":"lines 5049-5050","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF dashboard provides standardized country-year indicators with explicit 2022 values.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-period outcome evidence for 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect baseline/outcome evidence; not a single-policy claim.","contrary_evidence":["Water and sanitation coverage remain well below universal safely managed access, especially for sanitation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child-Related SDG Progress Assessment for Russian Federation","publisher":"UNICEF Data","publication_date":"2026-07-19","url":"https://data.unicef.org/sdgs/country/rus/","claim":"UNICEF's Russia dashboard reports 0% of children living in extreme poverty in 2022, 12% of children below the national poverty line in 2020, and 100% coverage of child social protection floors or systems in 2020.","locator":"lines 5050-5063","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF dashboard provides explicit indicators and years for child poverty and social protection coverage.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-period baseline and recent outcome context.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect outcome and system evidence; not a direct Putin act.","contrary_evidence":["The poverty figures are child-specific and the dashboard gives no 2022 national poverty line update here, so the picture is incomplete."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child-Related SDG Progress Assessment for Russian Federation","publisher":"UNICEF Data","publication_date":"2026-07-19","url":"https://data.unicef.org/sdgs/country/rus/","claim":"UNICEF's Russia dashboard reports 97% DTP3 immunization and 97% measles-first-dose coverage in 2022, 100% skilled birth attendance in 2020, maternal mortality of 14 per 100,000 live births in 2020, under-five mortality of 5 per 1,000 in 2021, and neonatal mortality of 2 per 1,000 in 2021.","locator":"lines 4943-4944","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF dashboard provides clear child-health indicators with explicit years and standardized definitions.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Mostly target-period-adjacent 2020-2022 outcome evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect system/outcome evidence; attribution to Putin is partial and mediated by long-run health institutions.","contrary_evidence":["These are outcome indicators rather than proof of 2022 policy improvement, and several values predate 2022 exactly."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian Federation (RUS) - Demographics, Health & Infant Mortality","publisher":"UNICEF Data","publication_date":"2026-07-19","url":"https://data.unicef.org/country/rus/","claim":"UNICEF's Russia country page reports an under-five mortality rate of 5 deaths per 1,000 live births, an infant mortality rate of 4, a neonatal mortality rate of 2, and a 2022 under-five total of 6,759 deaths.","locator":"lines 5333-5360","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF country page with explicit outcome values and a clear indicator table.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-period outcome context with 2022 values and immediately adjacent years.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect baseline/outcome evidence; not a direct policy act.","contrary_evidence":["Mortality remains low by international standards but not exceptional, and the page does not isolate Putin-period causal contribution."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian Federation (RUS) - Demographics, Health & Infant Mortality","publisher":"UNICEF Data","publication_date":"2026-07-19","url":"https://data.unicef.org/country/rus/","claim":"UNICEF's Russia country page reports 100% birth registration, 91% upper-secondary completion in 2020, 100% primary and lower-secondary completion in 2020, and 93% participation in organized early learning in 2019.","locator":"lines 5282-5289 and 5017-5043","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF dashboard supplies explicit child-protection and education-completion indicators with consistent country-year definitions.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Baseline and near-period education/protection context.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect system evidence; largely inherited and institution-mediated.","contrary_evidence":["Several indicators are pre-2022, so they are weaker as direct year-specific performance evidence."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian Federation (RUS) - Demographics, Health & Infant Mortality","publisher":"UNICEF Data","publication_date":"2026-07-19","url":"https://data.unicef.org/country/rus/","claim":"UNICEF's Russia country page reports 76% safely managed drinking water access, 97% basic drinking water access, 58% safely managed sanitation access, and 34% basic sanitation access.","locator":"lines 5640-5667","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF country page reports the water and sanitation coverage table with explicit country values.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-period outcome context.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect baseline/outcome evidence rather than direct Putin action.","contrary_evidence":["The sanitation split between safely managed and basic access shows a meaningful quality gap."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian Federation","publisher":"WHO","publication_date":"2026-07-19","url":"https://data.who.int/countries/643","claim":"WHO's Russia country page reports current health expenditure at 7.39% of GDP, doctor density at 51.1 per 10,000 population, and nursing and midwifery density at 70.4 per 10,000 population in 2022.","locator":"country profile summary / indicator cards","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO country profile provides direct indicator cards with explicit year and values.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-period 2022 health-system capacity context.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect, system-level evidence with long-run institutional attribution.","contrary_evidence":["The page also flags worsening nurse density since 2012, so the workforce picture is mixed."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rosstat представляет данные о естественном движении населения в декабре 2022 года","publisher":"Rosstat","publication_date":"2023-02-10","url":"https://rosstat.gov.ru/folder/313/document/197667","claim":"Rosstat reported that in 2022 Russia had 1,306,162 births, 1,905,778 deaths, and a natural decrease of 599,616 people, with deaths down 22.1% from 2021 and infant mortality down 17.7% in December 2022 compared with December 2021.","locator":"Rosstat announcement text / 2022 summary paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary national statistics release with explicit year-end figures and year-on-year comparisons.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 outcome evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect demographic outcome evidence; strongly confounded by pandemic and other structural factors.","contrary_evidence":["The country still recorded a large natural population decrease, so lower deaths did not translate into broad demographic improvement."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10"]}
